# Accessing a computed Var in a Base object · reflex-dev · Discussion #3205
created: 2024-05-01, last updated: 2026-04-16, [Read more](https://github.com/orgs/reflex-dev/discussions/3205)

@hpharmsen The `@rx.var` decorator only works within state classes (not within the a Base class as you have it here).
You want to use the `@property` decorator that's builtin to Python instead.
...
Here's a way to do this currently by overriding the `dict` method to make sure the property gets serialized:
```
import reflex as rx
class Person(rx.Base):
name: str
@property
def hello_name(self) -> str:
return "Hello " + self.name
def dict(self, **kwargs):
return super().dict(**kwargs) | {"hello_name": self.hello_name}
class State(rx.State):
count: int = 5
...
State.persons, lambda person: rx.html("<p>" + person.hello_name + "</p>")
)
...
Normally the way you had it would work since each field in the class will get sent to the frontend.
Pydantic doesn't by default send the `@property` tags down which is why we need this workaround.
We can probably try to make this happen by default so you don't need to modify the dict method.
...
Related to #2429, reflex could automatically serialize those properties without the `dict` workaround

